{
  "id": "04-gifts-for-service",
  "title": "Gifts for the Common Good",
  "tenet": "Receive and serve",
  "durationMs": 71160,
  "audioAsset": "kingdom/04-gifts-for-service.mp3",
  "transcriptAsset": "assets/kingdom/04-gifts-for-service.json",
  "text": "Who is meant to receive the good God has placed in your hands?\n\nA young woman carried warm bread to a neighbor whose husband had died. On the walk over, she rehearsed a speech about hope. At the door she saw the neighbor's tired face. She put the bread down, sat beside her, and listened. Only much later did she speak. The bread mattered. So did the hour of silence. Love had changed not only what she gave, but how she gave it.\n\nThe Spirit gives gifts for the good of others: discernment, faith, healing, prophecy, and strength to serve. We may ask for them, but cannot claim them as wages or turn another person's suffering into our stage. Even an ordinary gift needs humility, timing, and tenderness. The Spirit also grows patience, peace, and joy in people who keep walking with Christ. A strong impression in prayer still needs to be tested by Scripture, love, and wise counsel. Whose need is before you today? Ask God what would truly help. Perhaps it is a prayer, a meal, a truthful word, or quiet company. Offer what you have, and leave the outcome in his hands.",
